\chapter{N-player Go play revision}
\label{ch:gameplay}
The October 26 request asks to complete the KGS-style server: challenge
creation takes the board parameters of the stakeholder's N-Player Go Lab, and
seats not taken by people are played by Monte Carlo tree search bots running in
the server. This candidate extends the twelve-task specification with
\texttt{take\_seat}, \texttt{start\_game}, \texttt{play\_move} and
\texttt{game\_state}, and changes \texttt{create\_game} parameters. It answers
the open rules decisions of the requirements argument as follows.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Parameters.} Board 5, 7, 9, 11, 13 or 19 lines (default 11);
  2--8 seats (default 4); a human move deadline from 5 seconds to 64 minutes
  (default 10 seconds); a bot search time from 0.25 to 16 seconds (default
  1 second); and a configurable ko rule: Auto (on for two players, off for
  three or more), Off, or On.
  \item \textbf{Seats and bots.} Room members who open a challenge may take one
  empty seat each before the start. Only the creator starts the game; every seat
  still empty becomes a server bot named \texttt{bot-}\emph{seat}. A bot never
  moves for a person's seat, and a client cannot submit a bot's move.
  \item \textbf{Deadline.} An unanswered turn becomes a \emph{timeout}: it
  passes the turn but neither ends nor resets the passing round. A move that
  arrives after its deadline is rejected and the timeout wins. A full round of
  timeouts abandons the game, scored as it stands.
  \item \textbf{Territory.} An empty region bordered only by one colour's stones
  and the Wall belongs to that colour. Score is territory minus losses.
  \item \textbf{Ending.} The game ends when every remaining player has passed
  since the last stone, after a full round of timeouts, or at \(3N^2\) moves.
  A resigned seat passes automatically and cannot win. The server announces the
  final scores and winners in the game chat.
  \item \textbf{Ko.} When on, simple ko plus situational superko: a stone may not
  recreate an earlier board with the same seat to move. The Zobrist key only
  indexes candidates; equality is decided on the exact board and seat.
  \item \textbf{Deferred.} Team merges, handicaps, ratings, persistence, game
  record export and reconnect grace beyond reloading the page. Watchers may use
  game chat.
\end{itemize}

The positive invariants are that every board, clock and score shown comes from
one server-authoritative transition, and that every turn and every game ends.
Forbidden outcomes include a move for another seat or out of turn, two
transitions for one turn (move versus deadline versus bot result), a bot
result applied after its turn ended, an outsider reading or changing a game,
and unbounded bot computation. The implementation mapping binds bot results to
the game and turn revision, runs searches outside the game lock with a time
budget, a node cap and a bounded number of concurrent search slots, and covers
each forbidden outcome with a race-enabled test.

