\chapter{Lab parity revision: seats, teams, history and search}
\label{ch:lab-parity}
\section{Request}
On October 26 the stakeholder asked that the server game behave like the
N-Player Go Lab, the single-page prototype whose engine the server already
ports. The important behaviours are: choosing which seats are bots and which are
people; teams that people can merge; navigating the board history and playing
out alternatives that are not the actual game; and seeing the Monte Carlo tree
search estimations. The revision is also an exercise of the LeanFM method: book,
typed grammar and tasks, two gates, then a one-shot implementation. Everything
needed to continue lives in this book, \path{spec/}, \path{source/} and
\path{reports/}; the chat that produced it is disposable.

\section{Decisions recorded from the stakeholder}
Four questions were put to the stakeholder; each answer is now a requirement.
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Seat control.} The creator plans each seat as \emph{bot} or
  \emph{open} before the start. A person who holds a seat may switch
  \emph{autopilot} on (a server bot plays the seat) and off at any time during
  play; the seat stays theirs.
  \item \textbf{Teams.} The creator may preset teams, as in the Lab roster. During
  play two teams merge when every person seated in either team accepts; bots go
  along with their team's people; merges are permanent.
  \item \textbf{Analysis.} Watchers may branch and run local search at any time.
  Seated people may step through the actual moves during play, and may branch and
  search once the game has finished.
  \item \textbf{Search visibility.} Each bot move publishes its search summary as
  soon as it is played.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Derived rules}
\begin{itemize}
  \item A seat's team defaults to its seat number, so everyone plays alone unless
  the creator groups seats. Team numbers run from 1 to the seat count.
  \item A team with no seated person cannot be party to a merge: nobody could
  consent for it. A merged team takes the smaller team number.
  \item A proposal is withdrawn by any decline, by any change to either team, or
  by the game finishing. Only one proposal per pair of teams is pending.
  \item Team score is the sum of its members' territory minus the sum of their
  losses. Winners are the team or teams with the highest team score among teams
  with at least one member who has not resigned. Bots on a team search for the
  team's combined score (the Lab's team reward).
  \item Autopilot moves use the bot deadline and publish search summaries.
  Switching autopilot off during the seat's own turn discards the pending bot
  result; the person then has the rest of the human deadline measured from the
  start of the turn.
  \item Each removed stone is recorded against the seat that captured it, giving
  the Lab's per-capturer \emph{bowl}; suicide is recorded against the mover.
  \item Bot search settings are challenge parameters with the Lab's choices:
  exploration constant 0.5, 0.7 (default), 1.0 or 1.4; reward \emph{margin}
  (default), \emph{win} or \emph{rank}; margin softness 2, 4 (default) or 8.
\end{itemize}

\section{What every viewer sees}
The browser reproduces the Lab against server state:
\begin{itemize}
  \item the board with Wall coordinates, territory squares, the last-move marker,
  ghost markers for next moves, and a search heat overlay;
  \item navigation: Home, Back, Forward, End, previous and next variation, by
  buttons and by the arrow, Home and End keys; a variation tree; next-move chips;
  \item a \emph{Search at this position} panel: candidate moves with visit share
  and reward for the mover, from the bot's published summary or the viewer's own
  local search;
  \item a per-seat table with Territory, Bowl, Score, Proj.\ (projected final
  score) and Outlook (expected reward), and team totals;
  \item a clickable projected-score timeline along the viewed line;
  \item GIF export of the viewed line; stone, capture and final-ten-seconds sounds
  with a toggle;
  \item live following while the viewer is at the latest move; otherwise a notice
  that new moves arrived.
\end{itemize}
Variations and local searches run in the viewer's browser with the Lab engine,
are never sent to the server, and vanish when the page closes. This boundary is
a fairness convention enforced by the provided browser, not a security boundary:
anyone can copy a public position into another tool.

\section{Protocol shape}
New requests: \texttt{SetSeatPlan}, \texttt{LeaveSeat}, \texttt{SetAutopilot},
\texttt{ProposeMerge}, \texttt{RespondMerge}; \texttt{CreateGame} gains the bot
settings and a seat plan; \texttt{GetGameState} gains \texttt{since\_move} so a
viewer fetches only new move records. Server-originated events
(\texttt{BotMovePlayed}, \texttt{TurnTimedOut}, \texttt{AutoPassed},
\texttt{TeamsMerged}, \texttt{MergeWithdrawn}, \texttt{GameFinished}) reach game
members as entries of \texttt{GameState}. A third actor, \texttt{Bot}, is the
bounded pool of server search workers: the server sends \texttt{BotTurn} and
accepts \texttt{BotMoveChosen} only for the same game, seat and turn revision
before the deadline.

The grammar composes per-operation tasks into the \texttt{game\_lifecycle} task:
create, then any number of seat-plan, take, leave, join, chat and state
operations, then start, then any number of turns, autopilot switches, merges,
chats and state reads, then \texttt{GameFinished}. A \texttt{turn} is a person's
move, a bot turn, a timeout or an automatic pass. Positive obligations: every
turn ends exactly once; every started game finishes. Negative obligations cover
moving for another seat, double-ended turns, stale bot results, merges without
consent, analysis leaking into the game, and unbounded bot work.

\section{Deferred}
Handicaps, ratings, persistence across restarts, SGF export, push transport
(the browser polls), reconnect grace beyond reloading the page, and moderation.

\section{Implementation argument}
The separate mapping in \path{spec/Implementation.lean} binds the \texttt{Bot}
actor to a bounded worker pool, extends the Go engine's search to return the
Lab's summary (top candidates by visits; per seat the mean reward and mean final
score), keeps team arrays in the authoritative game, and serves the Lab engine
and search worker as same-origin static files so the browser can branch and
search locally under the existing content security policy (extended only with
same-origin workers and \texttt{blob:}/\texttt{data:} images for GIF preview).
Each negative obligation maps to a control and a race-enabled test listed in
\path{source/traceability.json}.

